# Maintenance / CMMS: the words around each step. Code is in steps21.py; build21.py runs it and writes the track.
TRACK = {
    "id": "t-maint",
    "name": "Maintenance / CMMS",
    "sub": "Maintenance numbers from the plant data: breakdowns against minor stops, MTBF and MTTR, inherent against measured availability, breakdowns nobody wrote up, PM compliance, planned against reactive hours, backlog in crew-weeks, repeat failures with no PM, cost per barrel and a KPI workbook",
    "files": {"sql": "maint.sql", "python": "maint.py"},
}
ILL = " The work orders, hours and their dates are illustrative; the faults are the canonical fault_log."
LESSONS = [
    ("Create work_order",
     "Create work_order: an identity key; machine; wo_type (PM, Corrective or Improvement); description; failure_mode and fault_id, both nullable; priority as a TINYINT; created, due and done times; estimated and actual hours as DECIMAL(5,2); and parts_cost, defaulting to 0.",
     "A CMMS (computerised maintenance management system) holds one row per job: what, on which machine, how urgent, when it was asked for, when it was due, when it was done, and the hours and parts it took. done_ts is NULL while a job is open, which is how every backlog query finds it. fault_id links a corrective job to the stop in fault_log, but there is no foreign key: the CMMS and the PLC log are usually separate systems, and a link that can be missing is exactly what step 5 checks. failure_mode says which failure a job is meant to fix or prevent." + ILL,
     [["wo_type", "Work order type", "PM (preventive), Corrective (fixing a failure) or Improvement."], ["failure_mode", "Failure mode", "The failure a job fixes or prevents, matching fault_log's fault names."], ["DEFAULT 0", "DEFAULT", "The value used when an insert leaves the column out."], ["TINYINT", "TINYINT", "A whole number from 0 to 255; enough for priority 1 to 4."]]),
    ("Breakdowns and minor stops",
     "For the OEE week, count each machine's stops of 5 minutes or more (breakdowns) and under 5 (minor stops), with the minutes of each, using CROSS APPLY to work out each fault's seconds once.",
     "Maintenance measures breakdowns: stops long enough to need a technician. Minor stops are usually cleared by the operator and belong to the TPM track's speed and minor-stop losses. The line between them is a convention, 5 minutes here as in the TPM track; set it from how your plant really responds. The minutes match the TPM track: breakdowns 85.97 / 47.32 / 80.45, minor stops 1.08 / 47.42 / 28.40. Conveyor 3 has the fewest breakdowns but the most minor stops.",
     [["CROSS APPLY", "CROSS APPLY (SELECT … AS secs)", "Works out a value once per row so the rest of the query can reuse it."], ["d.secs >= 300", "Breakdown", "5 minutes or more: the line between a breakdown and a minor stop."]]),
    ("MTBF and MTTR",
     "Work out each machine's operating minutes (9,450 planned minus all down minutes), MTBF (operating minutes per breakdown) and MTTR (breakdown minutes per breakdown), guarding the divisions with NULLIF.",
     "MTBF, mean time between failures, says how often a machine breaks; MTTR, mean time to repair, how long it takes to get it back. Char Station 2 and Hoop Driver 1 break about every 1,040 minutes, 17 running hours; Conveyor 3 every 2,339. Hoop Driver 1 is the quickest to fix, 8.94 minutes. Operating time takes off every stop, minor ones included, since the machine is not running during them either. A week is a short window for MTBF: four breakdowns is a small count, and one more would cut Conveyor 3's figure by a fifth, one fewer raise it by a third.",
     [["mtbf_min", "MTBF", "Mean time between failures: operating time per breakdown."], ["mttr_min", "MTTR", "Mean time to repair: repair minutes per breakdown."], ["NULLIF", "NULLIF(breakdowns, 0)", "Gives NULL instead of dividing by zero for a machine with no breakdowns."], ["@planned_min", "Planned time", "9,450 minutes: 7 days x 3 shifts x 450 paid minutes."]]),
    ("Two kinds of availability",
     "In Python, read the week's faults with ? parameters, rebuild MTBF and MTTR per machine, and compare inherent availability, MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR), with measured availability, operating over planned minutes. Print the gap in minutes.",
     "Inherent availability is what the machine's reliability and repair speed allow; it counts breakdowns only. Measured availability, the A in OEE, counts every stop. The gap between them is the minor stops: 47.18 minutes for Conveyor 3, almost all of its 47.42 (the formulas weight them slightly differently). When the gap is large, the machine is not losing time to breakdowns but to small stops, and the fix belongs to the operators and the process, not the maintenance crew. Both figures run about 99% here because fault_log holds only the PLC's jams, as the OEE track says. build21.py checks that these MTBF and MTTR figures equal the SQL step's.",
     [["inherent", "Inherent availability", "MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR): breakdowns only."], ["measured", "Measured availability", "Operating over planned time: every stop."], ["params=[WEEK_START, WEEK_END]", "params", "The ? values, passed safely."]]),
    ("Breakdowns with no work order",
     "List the OEE week's breakdowns that have no work order, using a LEFT JOIN to work_order and keeping the rows where the work order is missing.",
     "Every breakdown should have a work order, or the CMMS undercounts failures and MTBF from the CMMS looks better than the machine is. Two were never written up: a char flare and a misfeed, both just over the 5-minute line and one in the middle of the night. A LEFT JOIN keeps every fault and fills the work order's columns with NULL when there is none, so wo_id IS NULL finds the gaps. Run this weekly and the gaps get written up while people still remember them." + ILL,
     [["LEFT JOIN", "LEFT JOIN", "Keeps every fault, with NULLs where no work order matches."], ["w.wo_id IS NULL", "No match", "The fault has no work order."]]),
    ("PM compliance",
     "For each machine and in total, count the PMs due before now and those done on time, where on time means done by the due time plus 10% of the PM's interval (created to due), with the compliance percentage. Use ROLLUP for the total.",
     "PM compliance is the share of preventive jobs done when they were due. The grace window, 10% of the interval, is a common convention, not a standard: a weekly PM may be up to 16.8 hours late, a monthly one 3 days. Each PM is created one interval before it is due, so DATEDIFF of created and due is the interval. Conveyor 3's 20 October PM was done two days late; Hoop Driver 1's 3 November PM was never done, and a missing done_ts fails the comparison, so it counts as missed. 86.7% overall. PMs are done in the breaks between paid time in this sample, so they do not reduce the 9,450 planned minutes." + ILL,
     [["ROLLUP", "GROUP BY ROLLUP", "Adds a total row, with machine NULL."], ["ISNULL(machine, 'All')", "Label the total", "Names the ROLLUP row."], ["DATEADD", "DATEADD(MINUTE, …)", "The due time plus the grace window."]]),
    ("Planned and reactive hours",
     "For work orders done in the OEE week, split the actual hours into planned (PM and Improvement) and reactive (Corrective), with each one's share of the total using a window SUM.",
     "Reactive work is done when something breaks, on the machine's schedule; planned work on yours. Half the crew's hours went to breakdowns this week. A planned share of around 80% is a widely quoted benchmark for mature maintenance, not a standard, but 50% says the crew is mostly firefighting. SUM(SUM(act_hours)) OVER () is the total of the grouped sums, so each row can show its share in one query." + ILL,
     [["SUM(SUM(act_hours)) OVER ()", "Share of the total", "The grand total across the groups, for a percentage."], ["CASE WHEN wo_type = 'Corrective'", "Reactive", "Corrective jobs are reactive; the rest are planned."]]),
    ("Backlog in weeks",
     "Count the open work orders and their estimated hours as of 2026-11-07 06:00, and turn the hours into weeks of work at 12 crew hours a week.",
     "Backlog in hours means little on its own; backlog in crew-weeks says how long the open work would take with the time the crew actually has for these machines. 12 hours a week is an illustrative share of the crew's time. 27 hours is 2.25 weeks, inside the 2 to 4 weeks often quoted as healthy: much less and the crew may run out of planned work, much more and jobs wait too long. The number only means something if the estimates are honest.",
     [["@crew_hours_week", "Crew hours a week", "The hours the crew has for these machines (illustrative)."], ["done_ts IS NULL", "Open", "Not done yet."]]),
    ("Backlog aging",
     "List each open work order with its age in days as of 06:00 on 2026-11-07, an age bucket (0-7, 8-30, over 30), and whether it is past its due date, oldest first.",
     "A healthy total can hide a job nobody wants. The photo eye bracket has waited 50 days, past its due date by more than a month; at priority 4 it keeps losing to everything else, while Conveyor 3 had 13 jams this week, and a loose eye bracket is one cause of a missed or false jam. The gas train calibration and the missed weekly PM are overdue too. The Burner B igniter order is the one the 05:52 handoff note said had been opened.",
     [["DATEDIFF(DAY, created_ts, @now)", "Age", "Whole days since the job was asked for."], ["due_ts < @now", "Overdue", "Past its due date and still open."]]),
    ("Repeat failures",
     "For the OEE week, list each machine's faults that happened 3 or more times, with events and minutes, and count the PM and Improvement orders whose failure_mode targets that failure.",
     "A failure that keeps coming back needs prevention, not another repair. Hoop Driver 1's misfeeds happened 11 times for 61.23 minutes, and nothing preventive targets them: only repairs, and a sensor rebuild still open. Char flares have a monthly PM and an open calibration against them. The correlated subquery counts, for each repeat, the preventive orders aimed at it. The E-stops are operator safety actions rather than failures, so no PM belongs there; check why they were pressed." + ILL,
     [["HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3", "Repeat", "Kept only when it happened 3 or more times."], ["w.wo_type IN ('PM', 'Improvement')", "Preventive", "Jobs that prevent a failure rather than fix one."], ["(SELECT COUNT(*)", "Correlated subquery", "Runs once per row, using that row's machine and fault."]]),
    ("Before and after the PM",
     "Read the monthly PMs done in the OEE week, and for each machine count its breakdowns before and after its PM, with the days on each side and the rate per day.",
     "Does the PM prevent breakdowns? Char Station 2 dropped from 1.85 a day to 1.03; Conveyor 3 went from none to 0.83; Hoop Driver 1 barely moved. With 0 to 6 events on each side, one or two more or fewer would change every conclusion: these counts are too small to judge a PM. The check is still worth building, because over months of data it shows which PMs do nothing and can be cut, and which failures need a different task. All three monthly PMs were done at 10:00 on 2 November." + ILL,
     [["days_before", "Days before", "Time from the week start to the PM."], ["/ days_after", "Rate", "Breakdowns per day after the PM."], ["LIKE", "LIKE ?", "Matches descriptions starting with Monthly PM."]]),
    ("Maintenance cost per barrel",
     "For work orders done in the OEE week, total each machine's actual hours and parts, cost the hours at $48.00, add the parts, and divide by the week's barrels from v_hourly_barrels. Print the table and the all-machine cost per barrel.",
     "Cost per barrel puts maintenance in the same unit as everything else on the plant's P&L. The week cost $1,238.72 across 20,510 barrels, about 6 cents a barrel. Conveyor 3 is the most expensive, $0.1041, because of parts: a $104 photo eye and $336 of quick-release clamps (the clamps the SMED track uses, bought off contract in the Procurement track). The $48.00 loaded labour rate is illustrative; use your plant's. One week of maintenance cost moves a lot with one purchase, so trend it over months.",
     [["LABOUR_RATE", "Labour rate", "Loaded cost of a technician hour (illustrative)."], ["per_barrel", "Cost per barrel", "Labour and parts over barrels made."], ["v_hourly_barrels", "v_hourly_barrels", "The OEE track's view of barrels made each hour."]]),
    ("Weekly KPI workbook",
     "Work out PM compliance per machine in pandas, then write a Maintenance KPIs sheet with breakdowns, MTBF, MTTR, PM compliance, labour hours and cost per barrel per machine, and a totals row with SUM formulas. Save it, read it back and check the totals against pandas.",
     "One sheet a supervisor can read every Monday. Totals are SUM formulas, so the sheet still adds up if someone edits a cell; the read-back checks that the rows written add up to what pandas computed, 22 breakdowns and 16.64 hours. MTBF, MTTR and compliance are not summed: an average of averages would be wrong, so those cells are left empty in the totals row.",
     [["load_workbook", "load_workbook", "Opens a saved workbook to read it back."], ["=SUM(", "SUM formula", "A live total in the sheet."], ["compliance", "PM compliance", "Share of each machine's PMs done on time."]]),
    ("From repeat failure to PM task",
     "Price Hoop Driver 1's misfeeds for the week at its ideal rate and margin from dim_machine, then the saving at a 50% cut, the cost of a weekly half-hour feed-rail PM at the labour rate, and the net.",
     "The repeat-failure list said misfeeds have no preventive task. A weekly feed-rail check costs $24.00 a week of labour; misfeeds cost $2,385.55 a week, the TPM track's figure. At a 50% cut, the Savings Tracker's target for P2, that is $1,192.77 a week, $1,168.77 net. The baseline is P2's own, so this PM is P2's countermeasure, not a new saving: counting it again would double-count, as the Savings Tracker's checks would catch. The half-hour and the 50% are illustrative until the PM has run for a few weeks.",
     [["TARGET_CUT", "Target", "Share of the loss the PM should remove: P2's 50%."], ["PM_HOURS_WEEK", "PM time", "Half an hour a week (illustrative)."], ["ideal_rate_hr", "Ideal rate", "Barrels an hour the machine should make."]]),
]
